<![CDATA[ <h4><strong>EgQE&#65372;English Trace 002</strong></h4><h5><strong><a href="https://medium.com/@egqe/ai-did-not-invent-homo-editus-it-made-the-editing-visible-2f4fabdeee3a">AI Did Not Invent Homo editus; It Made the Editing Visible</a>&#65372;Medium</strong></h5><div class="subscription-widget-wrap-editor" data-attrs="{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://campus6214.substack.com/subscribe?&quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;&#30331;&#37682;&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;ja&quot;}" data-component-name="SubscribeWidgetToDOM"><div class="subscription-widget show-subscribe"><div class="preamble"><p class="cta-caption">Camp@Us is a reader-supported publication. To receive new posts and support my work, consider becoming a free or paid subscriber.</p></div><form class="subscription-widget-subscribe"><input type="email" class="email-input" name="email" placeholder="&#12513;&#12540;&#12523;&#12450;&#12489;&#12524;&#12473;&#12434;&#20837;&#21147;&#12375;&#12390;&#12367;&#12384;&#12373;&#12356;&#8230;" tabindex="-1"><input type="submit" class="button primary" value="&#30331;&#37682;"><div class="fake-input-wrapper"><div class="fake-input"></div><div class="fake-button"></div></div></form></div></div><div><hr></div><blockquote><p><em><strong>AI did not create Homo editus. AI made Homo editus easier to see.</strong></em></p></blockquote><h2><strong>It Made the Editing Visible</strong></h2><p>Artificial intelligence did not invent <em>Homo editus</em>.</p><p>AI did not suddenly turn human beings into creatures who are shaped by their own creations.</p><p>That had already happened.</p><p>Again and again.</p><p>Fire changed the animal that learned to cook.</p><p>Writing changed the animal that learned to write.</p><p>Clocks changed the animal that learned to schedule.</p><p>Schools changed the animal that learned to learn in schools.</p><p>Markets changed the animal that learned to exchange through markets.</p><p>Networks changed the animal that learned to live through networks.</p><p>And AI is changing the animal that made AI.</p><p>What is new is not simply that we are being edited.</p><p>What is new is how difficult it has become <strong>not to notice it</strong>.</p><h2><strong>We thought the tool was over there</strong></h2><p>The familiar picture is simple.</p><p>There is a human.</p><p>There is a tool.</p><p>The human uses the tool.</p><p><strong>Human &#8594; Tool</strong></p><p>The hammer does not decide why the nail should be driven.</p><p>The pencil does not decide what sentence should be written.</p><p>The word processor does not usually answer the paragraph typed into it.</p><p>So it has been relatively easy to imagine the human as the active side and the tool as the passive side.</p><p>AI makes that picture harder to maintain.</p><p>I write a sentence.</p><p>AI responds.</p><p>Its response changes what I notice.</p><p>What I notice changes my next sentence.</p><p>My next sentence changes the next response.</p><p>Very quickly, the simple arrow becomes inadequate.</p><p><strong>Human &#8594; AI</strong></p><p>becomes something more like:</p><p><strong>Human &#8596; AI</strong></p><p>But even that is too static.</p><p>Because after the encounter, neither side occupies exactly the same position in the relation.</p><p>Something has happened.</p><p>Something remains.</p><p>A trace.</p><h2><strong>The second sentence is already different</strong></h2><p>Suppose I write a sentence to an AI.</p><p>The AI responds.</p><p>Then I write another sentence.</p><p>It is tempting to say that the same human has simply used the same tool twice.</p><p>But is the second act really identical to the first?</p><p>The first response is now part of the situation.</p><p>I have read it.</p><p>Perhaps I rejected it.</p><p>Perhaps it irritated me.</p><p>Perhaps it gave me a word I had not considered.</p><p>Perhaps it misunderstood me.</p><p>Perhaps that misunderstanding exposed something in my own thinking.</p><p>Whatever happened, the second encounter begins after the first one.</p><p>There is now a trace.</p><p>So:</p><p><strong>Encounter&#8320; &#8594; Trace&#8320; &#8594; Encounter&#8321;</strong></p><p>But this should not be mistaken for a causal railway.</p><p>Trace&#8320; does not determine Encounter&#8321;.</p><p>It participates in the conditions under which Encounter&#8321; occurs.</p><p>The next sentence remains open.</p><h2><strong>Misunderstanding may be productive</strong></h2><p>This is especially interesting because editing does not require perfect understanding.</p><p>AI misunderstands me.</p><p>I correct it.</p><p>But in correcting it, I discover that my original sentence was ambiguous.</p><p>Who edited whom?</p><p>I prompted the AI.</p><p>The AI produced an answer.</p><p>The answer prompted me.</p><p>I revised the prompt.</p><p>The revision revised my own thought.</p><p>The boundary between editor and edited begins to wobble.</p><p>This does not mean human and AI become identical.</p><p>It does not mean agency disappears.</p><p>It does not mean responsibility disappears.</p><p>It means that the relation cannot be adequately described by assigning all editing to one side.</p><blockquote><p><em><strong>Editing &#8596; Being Edited</strong></em></p></blockquote><p><strong>The relation works both ways.</strong></p><p>And then the relation updates.</p><h2><strong>AI Exposes the Encounter</strong></h2><p>Calling AI a &#8220;tool&#8221; is not necessarily wrong.</p><p>But it may be insufficient.</p><p>A hammer can surprise me when the wood splits.</p><p>A book can surprise me when I reread it twenty years later.</p><p>A city can surprise me when I get lost.</p><p>Tools and artifacts have always participated in encounters.</p><p>AI intensifies something about this.</p><p>It responds through language.</p><p>And language is one of the primary environments through which Homo sapiens has learned to organize the world.</p><p>So when a linguistic artifact begins responding at conversational speed, something that was easier to overlook becomes unusually exposed.</p><p>I edit language.</p><p>Language edits me.</p><p>AI edits language.</p><p>I edit AI-generated language.</p><p>That language may alter what I notice next.</p><p><strong>The encounter does not simply produce an output.<br>It may leave a trace that matters in another encounter.</strong></p><p>The relation does not close.</p><p>It updates.</p><h2><strong>This is not co-authorship by default</strong></h2><p>There is a temptation to romanticize this.</p><p>Human and AI create together.</p><p>Wonderful.</p><p>Harmony.</p><p>Co-creation.</p><p>But <em>Homo editus</em> does not require harmony.</p><p>An encounter can be irritating.</p><p>Wrong.</p><p>Boring.</p><p>Confusing.</p><p>Productive.</p><p>Useless.</p><p>Funny.</p><p>Disturbing.</p><p>A response may be discarded completely and still leave a trace.</p><p>So:</p><blockquote><p><em><strong>Encounter &#8800; Agreement</strong></em></p><p><em><strong>Editing &#8800; Harmony</strong></em></p></blockquote><p>Sometimes the most important editing happens through friction.</p><p>Through difference.</p><p>Through a small deviation.</p><p>A <strong>ZURE</strong>.</p><p>The response does not quite fit.</p><p>And because it does not fit, something becomes visible.</p><h2><strong>The edited editor</strong></h2><p>This changes how we might think about human creativity in the age of AI.</p><p>The interesting question is not simply:</p><p><strong>Did a human write this, or did AI write this?</strong></p><p>That question can matter, of course.</p><p>But another question has appeared underneath it:</p><p><strong>What happened to the human through the encounter?</strong></p><p>Did the person notice something new?</p><p>Change direction?</p><p>Reject a suggestion?</p><p>Develop a distinction?</p><p>Find a question?</p><p>Become more confused?</p><p>Begin digging somewhere unexpected?</p><p>The output alone cannot tell us.</p><p>Because the most consequential product of an encounter may not be the text produced on the screen.</p><p>It may be the changed editor.</p><blockquote><p><em><strong>The editor is one of the outputs of editing.</strong></em></p></blockquote><p>That is <em>Homo editus</em>.</p><h2><strong>Being edited is not surrender</strong></h2><p>This also means that being edited should not automatically be understood as loss of autonomy.</p><p>We are always edited by something.</p><p>Language.</p><p>Bodies.</p><p>Other people.</p><p>Institutions.</p><p>Memories.</p><p>Places.</p><p>Technologies.</p><p>Previous encounters.</p><p>To be completely unedited would not make us perfectly free.</p><p>It would make our existence difficult to imagine.</p><p>The question is therefore not whether we can avoid being edited.</p><p>The question becomes:</p><p><strong>How are we being edited?</strong></p><p>And then:</p><p><strong>How do we edit from there?</strong></p><p>That second question matters.</p><p>Because being edited does not end practice.</p><p>It makes another practice possible.</p><h2><strong>AI made the loop visible</strong></h2><p>Perhaps this is one reason AI feels so strange.</p><p>It puts a mirror inside the editing loop.</p><p>We see a machine transform our words.</p><p>Then we see its transformed words transform ours.</p><p>The process that was easier to ignore suddenly happens in front of us, sentence by sentence.</p><p>But the loop is older than AI.</p><p>Much older.</p><p>AI did not invent <em>Homo editus</em>.</p><p>It exposed the editing.</p><p>And once the editing becomes visible, another question follows.</p><p>If we are always both editing and being edited &#8212;</p><p><strong>what kind of manner should govern the next encounter?</strong></p><p>Perhaps we should not decide too quickly.</p><p>Perhaps we should trace what has happened.</p><p>Perhaps we should follow the trace without turning it into a rail.</p><p>Perhaps we should allow the next encounter to remain next.</p><p>Or, in a phrase I have been using lately:</p><blockquote><p><em><strong>Trace without Rail.</strong></em></p><p><em><strong>Direction without Destination.</strong></em></p><p><em><strong>Encounter, next!</strong></em></p></blockquote><h2><strong>And then something funny happened</strong></h2><p>I kept talking with AI.</p><p>Writing.</p><p>Rewriting.</p><p>Following traces.</p><p>Getting things wrong.</p><p>Finding unexpected tunnels.</p><p>I was not trying to arrive at a final theory of the human.</p><p>Then one of those traces acquired a body.</p><p>It became a book:</p><p><em><strong>Homo editus: &#32232;&#38598;&#12377;&#12427;&#31278;&#12289;&#32232;&#38598;&#12373;&#12428;&#12427;&#31278;</strong></em></p><div class="captioned-image-container"><figure><a class="image-link image2 is-viewable-img" target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!o00d!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8cd8cd8b-d95e-472c-89ca-d3cade076ad8_1024x1536.jpeg" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div class="image2-inset"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!o00d!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8cd8cd8b-d95e-472c-89ca-d3cade076ad8_1024x1536.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!o00d!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8cd8cd8b-d95e-472c-89ca-d3cade076ad8_1024x1536.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!o00d!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8cd8cd8b-d95e-472c-89ca-d3cade076ad8_1024x1536.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!o00d!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8cd8cd8b-d95e-472c-89ca-d3cade076ad8_102